The page finishes the velaria (sail curve) from Problem II and sets up Problem III, the elastica.

**(1) Velaria.** The wind load is normal to the curve and proportional to cos²φ, so p = 0 and q = −β cos²φ. The previous page derived:
- tension T = C (constant);
- C·dφ/cos²φ = β ds;
- C/cos φ = βx + D;
- dy = C dx / √((βx+D)² − C²);
- βs = √((βx+D)² − C²) + E.

Euler now puts the origin at the middle A of the sail, where the tangent is vertical. This forces C = D, and he sets C = D = βa. Then:
- dy = a dx / √(2ax + x²);
- y = a·ln[(x + a + √(2ax + x²))/a];
- arc length s = √(2ax + x²);
- tension βa, the same at every point.

This is the ordinary catenary of parameter a, with the roles of the axes exchanged relative to the hanging chain. Euler therefore says that everything noted earlier about the catenary applies here too. This recovers Jakob/Johann Bernoulli's identification of the velaria with the catenary.

**(2) Problem III: the elastica.** A uniform, weightless, equally elastic filament is bent by arbitrary forces applied only at its ends A and B. Since there are no distributed loads, p = q = 0. The problem belongs to the paper's 'second case', where the filament resists bending. Euler writes:
- constitutive law: Vv = A dφ/ds, i.e. bending moment = stiffness × curvature;
- I. dT + V dφ = 0;
- II. dV − T dφ = 0;
- III. d(Vv) = V ds.

Here T is the tangential force (tension), V the normal (shear) force, and Vv the moment. In modern form these are the planar equilibrium equations of an inextensible rod with shear: T′ + κV = 0, V′ − κT = 0, M′ = V, and M = Aκ.

On the next page Euler eliminates variables to get V = A φ″, and then:
- T = B − Aκ²/2;
- an elliptic quadrature ds = du/√(C + (4B/A)u² − u⁴) for the curvature u;
- by a second, easier route, T² + V² = const, giving T = C cos(φ + D) and V = C sin(φ + D). This is the classical elastica, with a constant resultant end force.
